\section{The prior on a when the reference is far from the failures}{

For the rates anchored at the reference temperature (\code{exp}, \code{zeroexp},
\code{zeropower}, \code{power}), a is the rate at \code{Tr}, or its scale there, and \code{Tr} may lie
a distance d = T_f - Tr from where failures were observed. The log rate at T_f is
then log a plus a term in b and d (b d for \code{exp}), so log a is the log rate at
T_f less that term, and it inherits the uncertainty in b across the distance:
\itemize{
\item its centre is the centre at T_f less the term at the prior median of b;
\item its variance is 2^2 plus the square of (the change in the term per unit of b,
times the prior standard deviation of b).
}

For \code{exp} with the default prior on \code{logb} this is
\verb{normal(centre - 0.5 d, sqrt(4 + (1.07 d)^2))}: the usual prior when the
reference is at the failures (d = 0), and a much wider one 14 degrees below them.
A prior of standard deviation 2 on log a at a distant reference would in effect
be a tight prior on b, and biases it. The calculation uses the prior on \code{logb} in
force: the one given in \code{intercept_priors} when it is a normal, normal(mu, s),
whose b has median exp(mu) and standard deviation
sqrt((exp(s^2) - 1) exp(2 mu + s^2)); the default for any other.

The threshold functions are anchored at \code{Tc}, not \code{Tr}, so for them d is the
distance from the centre of the prior on \code{Tc} in force to the failures. The default
prior on \code{Tc} is centred at T_f, and so the centre of log a stays at T_f; so it
does for a prior on \code{Tc} centred above the failures, where the rate is held near
zero. However, \code{Tc} may lie anywhere in its prior, and well above \code{Tc} the data
fix only log a less the function's term at the distance from \code{Tc} to the failures
(log a - b \code{Tc} for \code{exp_threshold}). A prior of standard deviation 2 on log a
would then hold \code{Tc} close to the failures with a confidence the data do not give:
in simulations with \code{Tc} 4 degrees below the failures it placed \code{Tc} about 1.2
degrees too high, with intervals that never covered the truth. Its variance
therefore adds (slope b_hi)^2, where slope is the change per unit of b of the
function's term at the reach of the prior on \code{Tc} in force, half the width of its
central 95\% interval (1.96 times the standard deviation of a normal, 3 by default,
or the 97.5\% quantile of a Student-t times its scale; the default reach for any
other prior), and b_hi the 97.5\% quantile of the prior on b (about 3.5 by
default): with the defaults, about 21 for \code{exp_threshold}, 22 for
\code{zeroexp_threshold} and 6.6 for \code{zeropower_threshold}. \code{const_threshold} has no b
and keeps 2. Where the data do place \code{Tc}, the wider prior changes nothing; where
they do not, the intervals on \code{Tc} and log a are as wide as the data leave them,
and the fit may take longer.
}

\section{Groups whose failures lie far apart}{

The intercept prior on log a is centred at the failure temperature pooled over all
individuals. Where log a differs between groups (species, say, or acclimation
temperatures), a group's coefficient on log a is about -b times its distance in
sCTmax from the reference group, so that with a steep b a tight prior holds it
back. The result is a biased b and baseline shape while sCTmax looks right: in
simulations of a three-species design with a Weibull shape of 4, the default
priors of version 0.2.1 biased the shape of the reference species by about -0.7.

Since version 0.2.2 the defaults on log a are therefore scaled to the groups,
which removed that bias in the same simulations. The rule needs a b to
extrapolate with, so \code{const} and \code{const_threshold} keep the priors of the section
above for every term. A term of the formula of log a marks groups when each of
its variables is a factor, a character or logical column, or a numeric column
with two values, alone or in an interaction. Where there is such a term, with
b_hi the 97.5\% quantile of the prior on b (exp(-0.7 + 1.96), about 3.5, by
default; read from \code{intercept_priors} when the prior on \code{logb} given there is a
normal):
\itemize{
\item the intercept keeps its centre and takes standard deviation
sqrt(2^2 + (d_g b_hi)^2), where d_g is the largest distance from \code{Tr} of any
group's median failure temperature, a group being a level of one variable or a
combination of levels of an interaction (for the exponential function; for the
other functions anchored at \code{Tr}, the change per unit of b of the function's
term at that distance, as in the section above). For a threshold function,
anchored at \code{Tc}, the distance from \code{Tr} plays no part and the intercept takes
the standard deviation of the section above;
\item each group term's coefficients take \code{normal(0, s)}, with
s = sqrt(1 + (b_hi max(D, 1))^2), where D is the range in degrees of the median
failure temperatures over the term's groups (its levels, or the combinations of
levels of an interaction). The floor of 1 degree is there because a median
mixes design with effect: a treatment given only in the ramps can lower
tolerance while its median failure temperature barely moves. s is a prior on
the difference between groups, which is what the coefficient of a factor or of
a 0/1 column is; the coefficient of a numeric column with two other values is
per unit of the column (per percentage point of oxygen coded 30 and 100), so s
is divided by the gap between the two values, and across an interaction by the
product of such gaps. The model and its coefficients stay in the units of the
data. A group none of whose individuals failed has no median failure
temperature and is left out of the rule, so its coefficient takes the width the
other groups set among themselves; where such a group may be much more
tolerant than the others, widen its prior through \code{slope_prior};
\item a continuous covariate keeps the slope prior.
}

A group term takes the wider of this prior and the \code{slope_prior} that applies to
log a, so that setting \code{slope_prior} for the other terms never narrows it; a
\code{slope_prior} whose scale cannot be read (other than a normal, a Student-t or the
flat prior \code{""}) gives way to it. A prior named for \code{loga} in \code{intercept_priors}
replaces the rule for the intercept. A group term given a narrower prior than the
rule's must be set through a full \code{prior} passed to \code{\link[=fit_hazard]{fit_hazard()}}. To widen
further:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{build_hazard_prior(..., intercept_priors = list(loga = "normal(-0.8, 20)"),
                   slope_prior = list(loga = "normal(0, 20)"))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Whether the priors of a fitted model pull its estimates, with these defaults or
any others, can be checked without refitting with \code{\link[=check_prior]{check_prior()}}.
}

\section{The baseline term c of the power function}{

In M(T) = c + a (T - Tr)^b, c is the rate that remains at the reference
temperature: failure that happens whatever the warming. Its default is centred so
that about 1\% of individuals would fail over \code{tau_ref} for reasons unrelated to
temperature, \code{normal(log(0.01) - log(tau_ref), 2)} with the memoryless or Gompertz
baseline and \code{normal(log(0.01), 2)} otherwise, which covers background failure
from about 0.02\% to 40\%. The 1\% is a judgement, not a measured value. Where
controls were held at the reference temperature, set it from them: if a fraction q
of controls failed over a time t, c is about -log(1 - q) / t, so for instance
\code{intercept_priors = list(logc = "normal(log(-log(1 - 0.02) / 120), 1)")} for 1 in
50 failing over 120 minutes.
}

\examples{
# A ramp from 20 to 35 degrees over 60 minutes, with two failures.
path   <- ramp_path("A", T_start = 20, rate = 0.25, t_max = 60)
checks <- data.frame(path_id = "A", time = seq(0, 60, by = 1))
ind    <- data.frame(id = 1:3, path_id = "A", event = c(1, 1, 0),
                     t_upper = c(30, 40, 60), acclim = c(10, 12.5, 15))
long   <- build_hazard_data(ind, path, "interval", covariates = "acclim",
                            checks = checks)

# Default weakly-informative priors for an exponential-threshold + Weibull model.
build_hazard_prior("exp_threshold", baseline = "weibull", long = long)

# Override a couple of priors (a cooler species, a tighter amplitude) and narrow
# the slope prior of log a; every other parameter keeps its default.
build_hazard_prior("exp_threshold", baseline = "weibull",
  dpar_formulas = list(loga = ~ 1 + acclim),
  intercept_priors = list(Tc = "normal(26, 4)", loga = "normal(-6, 1.5)"),
  slope_prior = list(loga = "normal(0, 0.5)"), long = long)

# Two acclimation groups: log a has a group term, and its priors are scaled to
# how far apart the groups fail.
ind$acclim <- c(10, 15, 15)
long <- build_hazard_data(ind, path, "interval", covariates = "acclim",
                          checks = checks)
build_hazard_prior("exp", dpar_formulas = list(loga = ~ 1 + acclim), long = long)
}
